  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather today through late this\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nClear skies persist into the overnight hours tonight, followed\nby a weak cold front crossing the forecast area late tonight\ninto early Tuesday morning. Though some clouds may be associated\nwith the front, the region is expected to remain dry with this\nfeature, with the most notable change being a northerly shift in\nwind direction by daybreak. Some patchy fog is possible, but\nshould any develop, it will most likely do so in more sheltered\nriver valleys where winds remain calm through the early morning\nhours.\n\nMixing heights may increase to around 900 mb by Tuesday\nafternoon, allowing for the boundary layer to dry out and\nrelative humidity to drop into the upper 40s to lower 50s. For\nTuesday night, a return of fog could be in the cards, but\ncurrent NAM forecast profiles suggest that if anything it will\nbe more of a low stratus deck, most likely obscuring skies in \nthe North Woods but potentially throughout the Crown of Maine \ninto early Wednesday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Nor'easter could potentially cause minor beach \nerosion to the coast from Tuesday night into possibly Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cutoff low off the Mid Atlantic Coast Wednesday morning lifts\nnortheast through Thursday morning, then either continues \nnortheast into The Maritimes or heads eastward Friday.\n\nEnsemble guidance generally keeps the storm well offshore but a\nfew solutions bring it further north towards our area. If the \nstronger more northern solutions for this Nor'easter are \ncorrect, then there could be some minor beach erosion along \nportions of the Downeast coast around the time of high tide from\nTuesday night into at least Thursday. While predicted water \nlevels will remain below coastal flood benchmarks, there could \nbe sufficient wave setup/runup over multiple high tide cycles to\ncause some minor beach erosion at more vulnerable locations \nalong the coasts of Hancock and Washington County. With the vast\nmajority of solutions with the Nor'easter not favoring a \nstronger and more northward track at this time, the current \nchance for even minor beach erosion with this system is very \nlow, at 10-20%.",
